---
import Section from './Section.astro';

// How the team grows, and what the current round reaches. The count is looked at
// beside the fleet, which is what makes the small numbers honest.
const phases = [
  {
    phase: '1',
    when: 'Q4 2026 to Q1 2027',
    what: 'Ship the deterministic engine and the first pilot deployments. Set up engineering, and begin talks with design partners.',
    hiring: '2 engineers',
  },
  {
    phase: '2',
    when: 'Q1 to Q2 2027',
    what: 'First paid deployments. Expand the engine to a second class of system.',
    hiring: '2 engineers, 1 partnerships',
  },
  {
    phase: '3',
    when: 'Q3 2027',
    what: 'Regional expansion. First IP licensing talks with a company running AI at the edge.',
    hiring: '3 engineers',
  },
];

const milestones = [
  'Ktema Systems incorporated, within 12 months.',
  'The record format complete at version 0.1.',
  'The first design partner signed, with the engine entering their product.',
];
---

<Section
  id="company"
  label="Company"
  title="Ktema Systems"
  lede="Ktema Systems is the name of the venture, and no company exists today. The jurisdiction of incorporation is not fixed yet, and it will be decided with the lead investor."
>
  <div class="stack">
    <h3>What the entity holds</h3>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The entity will hold the engine and its implementations, the hardware kernels, the
        conformance suite, and the marks. It will sell the licensed build with the certification
        included, and license the decoder to silicon vendors later.
      </p>
      <p>
        The format and the research layers beneath the products are documented, so a conforming
        implementation can recompute an address and a deployment can be checked independently. The
        conformance suite is the specification a second implementation has to pass, and
        conformance runs through the company.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="stack">
    <h3>The team</h3>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The fleet already runs for our own agentic development process: dozens of lightweight
        agent instances on a single host. The hires below are the engineers who work alongside it,
        and the team is built around the founder's industry experience across Korea, Europe, and
        beyond.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="data">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Phase</th>
            <th>When</th>
            <th>What</th>
            <th>Hiring</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            phases.map((row) => (
              <tr>
                <td class="lead">{row.phase}</td>
                <td>{row.when}</td>
                <td>{row.what}</td>
                <td>{row.hiring}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="stack">
    <h3>The round</h3>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The company is raising a pre-seed round. It funds the commercial layer and the engineering
        items the first design partner needs: tighter memory bounds, a smaller footprint in flash,
        and a second independent implementation. The instrument and the terms are expected to be
        set with the lead investor. That round reaches three milestones:
      </p>
    </div>
    <ul class="points">
      {milestones.map((line) => <li>{line}</li>)}
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="stack">
    <h3>What comes next</h3>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Ktema is the first product, Delta is the platform, and the rest follow them into the same
        deployments. The endgame is a system that can account for its own history, with no server in
        the path. A natural long-term commercial extension is space systems: the record has to stay on
        the machine, and the qualification path is the work.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Section>

<style>
  .stack > * + * {
    margin-top: 18px;
  }
</style>
